  "query_preview": "..\n(This afternoon through next Friday)\n\nWEATHER PATTERN OVERVIEW: \n\nOne final day of quiet weather before changes to the weather \npattern start another period of active weather. The rest of black \nFriday looks to be quiet with above normal temperatures and light \nwinds. However, by tonight into Saturday morning an upper trough \nwill pass through the Pacific NW, but just close enough to clip \nnorthern Nevada. The main impact of this system will be to higher \nelevation winds for Elko and Humboldt counties, with SW winds \nreaching 20 to 30 MPH with gusts up to 45 MPH possible early \nSaturday morning. Timing for the strongest winds look to occur \nbetween midnight an 10 AM, which may keep the strongest winds from\nmixing down to the passes and Valleys. Precipitation chances with\nthis clipper will be limited to along and north of the I-80 \ncorridor, with a 10% to 20% chance for isolated showers, further \nnorth along the ID/OR border chances rise to 20% to 40% for \nisolated to scattered showers. Snow levels have trended higher \nwith this system, starting at about 7500 feet, before falling to \nnear 6000 feet by noon Saturday. This will keep any snow to the \nmountain summits of northern Elko and far NE Humboldt county and \naway from roadways, with the exception of NV 225 between Wildhorse\nRes. and Mountain City. Amounts will be light with up to a 0.10\u201d \nof rain, and 1\u201d to 4\u201d of snow for the mountain peaks along and \nnorth of I-80. Saturday night through Sunday afternoon be a quiet \nas a fast moving shortwave ridge follows in the wake of the \ntrough. Sunday night through Thursday, will see a series of \nPacific troughs take aim at the west, which will bring several \nrounds of widespread valley rain and mountain snow chances back to\nthe Great basin. Models are showing multiple pieces of energy \nfrom up to 4 different troughs. The first system on Sunday night \ninto Monday looks to be the weak, and primarily affecting northern\nNevada, with isolated valley rain and mountain snow showers with \nlight accumulations possible with up to 0.10\u201d of rain and 1\u201d to 3\u201d\nof snow for the peaks. The second system will primarily affect \ncentral Nevada Tuesday morning through Wednesday morning, and \nlooks to be stronger than the first and slightly cooler, with snow\nlevels around 5500 feet for N NV, and 6000 feet for Central NV. \nSo still a mix of valley rain mountain snow for the end of the \nweek, as for amounts, N NV looks to get between 0.05\u201d up to 0.25\u201d \nof liquid, with up to 1\u201d to 4\u201d of new snow for the range peaks. \nCentral NV looks to get between 0.10\u201d to 0.75\u201d of water with a \ntrace to 3\u201d of snow for the passes of US-50, up to 3\u201d to 8\u201d of \nsnow for the highest passes and range summits. The Next system \nmodels are still rather uncertain with as to location and timing, \nas it look to begin moving through Thursday into Friday, of next \nweek with a track at this time favors southern and central NV but \nconfidence is still low for this system. Temperatures for now look\nto hold in the mid 40s to mid 50s for highs, and with lows \nholding in the mid 20s to mid 30s.\n\nFORECAST CONFIDENCE & DEVIATIONS: \n \nThere is high confidence in quiet weather pattern lasting through\nFriday of this week with high pressure dominating.\n\nThere is medium confidence in a quick moving upper trough passing\nthrough the Pacific NW bringing a low 10% to 40% chance of \nisolated to scattered valley rain and mountain snow showers for \nnorthern Nevada Saturday.\n\nThere is high confidence in a series of upper level troughs \nmoving through Nevada beginning Monday that will bring periods of \nrain and snow over the following few days. However confidence is \nmedium to low on potential impacts after Wednesday.",
